To keep your Ubuntu laptop awake and running bug bounty automation scripts even when you close the lid, you need to adjust the system’s backend rules. [1] 
By default, Ubuntu forces laptops to go to sleep the moment the lid closes. Since you already applied the general screen settings, follow these steps to stop the lid-close sleep behavior: [2] 
## Adjusting the Systemd Configuration (Permanent Fix)
Ubuntu manages the laptop lid switch using a core service called systemd-logind. You can configure it to completely ignore when the lid closes: [2, 3] 

   1. Open the system configuration file in your terminal using the nano text editor:
   
   sudo nano /etc/systemd/logind.conf
   
   [4] 
   2. Find these specific lines (they usually have a # symbol in front of them, which means they are commented out):
   
   #HandleLidSwitch=suspend
   #HandleLidSwitchExternalPower=suspend
   
   [4, 5] 
   3. Modify the lines by removing the # and changing the value to ignore:
   
   HandleLidSwitch=ignore
   HandleLidSwitchExternalPower=ignore
   
   (Note: This tells Ubuntu to "do nothing" when you close the lid, keeping your processes running whether you are on battery or plugged into the wall.) [4, 5, 6] 
   4. Save and exit the editor:
   * Press Ctrl + O and hit Enter to write the changes.
      * Press Ctrl + X to close the editor. [4, 6] 
   5. Apply the changes immediately by restarting the login service:
   
   sudo systemctl restart systemd-logind
   
   ⚠️ Heads up: Running this command might briefly log you out of your current desktop session. Make sure your current Git progress or scripts are saved before executing it. [2, 4] 

